s to the  thesis first part is that the gold  standard and I quote Doug Irwin from  Dartmouth as to what the thesis is the  gold standard was the key factor in the  origins of the Great Depression so  that's part one and part two is a key  factor in the transmission of the Great  Depression from country to country so  let me first deal with the deflation  question in the United States this is a  picture of the paper currency that was  issued in Great Britain it's actually a  10 shilling note which is a pretty small  denomination during the First World War  and the real problem of the deflation  well to understand the deflation in the  30s we have to go we have to talk about  the interwar period as a whole you've  already heard a little about it and how  it wasn't really a true gold standard it  was a jerry-rigged gold exchange system  and to understand the interwar period we  have to go back to World War one and I  put most of the blame for the interwar  deflation on the first world wars  monetary experience which was inflation  of course countries went off the gold  standard in order to print money in  order to pay their war bills the great  economist Leland Yeager was once to  asked would you recommend going back on  a gold standard  and he said yes I would but I would also  recommend that well while we're doing it  we repeal the First World War and that's  kind of my position too but what  happened in the First World War was as I  said the government's issued a lot of  money in the in Great Britain the price  level basically doubled so at the end of  the war you've got a pound which buys  half as much as the amount of gold that  the pound used to be equivalent to still  buys all right so how do you bring those  two things back into alignment you have  a choice you either cut the gold content  of the pound or if you want to keep the  gold content of the pound you're gonna  have to bring the price level back down  which means you have to go through a  major major d